---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// The living AI policy. The essay at /blog/how-i-use-ai is where these rules
// came from and stays frozen as a dated post; THIS page is what is currently
// true. Rule changes land in the log at the bottom and old entries stay.
// The aiRole chip on every post links here, so this page is the answer to
// "according to what standard?" at the moment a reader asks it.
const version = 'v1.0';
const updated = '2026-08-18';

const tags = [
  {
    label: 'written by hand',
    body: 'I wrote the outline and every sentence. A model answered questions and checked facts, and wrote none of it.',
  },
  {
    label: 'research assist',
    body: 'I wrote the outline and every sentence, and a model gathered or verified source material that shaped the content.',
  },
  {
    label: 'ai-drafted, edited by me',
    body: 'Drafted to an outline I argued over first, against a large body of my own prior writing, then edited line by line until it said what I meant.',
  },
];

const log = [
  {
    version: 'v1.0',
    date: '2026-08-18',
    body: 'First version. Replaced the aiAssisted boolean, which was true on all nine published posts and therefore said nothing, with the three-level tag. Backfilled every published post to "ai-drafted, edited by me" and added the per-post process notes.',
  },
];
---

<Layout title="AI policy · ProbablyFine" description="What the AI tag on every post means, where the lines are, and what happens when I get it wrong. Versioned, with a changelog.">
  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-shell)] mx-auto px-6 pt-16 pb-8">
    <p class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]">$ cat ./ai-policy</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-3">AI policy</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      This is the living version of the rules argued for in{' '}
      <a href="/blog/how-i-use-ai" class="hover:text-[var(--color-ink)]">How I use AI</a>.
      The post is where they came from and stays as written. This page is what's currently true.
      When a rule changes, the change lands in the log at the bottom and the old entry stays visible.
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-4">{version} · updated {updated}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-[var(--container-shell)] mx-auto px-6 pb-8">
    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># scope</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        This covers writing I publish under my own name: this blog, and the copies of posts that go
        out to social surfaces. It doesn't cover project documentation, which is mostly generated
        from the code and reviewed the way code is. It doesn't cover my day job, which isn't mine
        to write about.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># the tag</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Every post carries one of three labels:
      </p>
      {
        tags.map((t) => (
          <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-ink)]">{t.label}</span> · {t.body}
          </p>
        ))
      }
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        The labels are for scanning. Every post also carries a note at the bottom describing what
        happened to that particular piece, and the note is the real disclosure.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Where that stands today: every post published so far is tagged "ai-drafted, edited by me,"
        so the labels don't distinguish anything yet. They start working when the first hand-written
        piece ships, and one is planned.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># the lines</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Writing gets the tag and the note. The standard doesn't change between categories, only the
        method does.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Engineering uses AI heavily. I direct it, review what comes back, reject a lot of it, and
        I'm responsible for all of it. I red-team what I ship, especially the parts a model wrote,
        because those are the parts most likely to look right and be wrong.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-3 leading-relaxed">
        Art, music, and voice: no generated assets in anything I ship. Narrative prose is held to
        the same standard as the rest of my writing. Given the option on anything, I'll use
        human-created, every time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># when I get it wrong</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        A mislabeled post gets corrected in place, and the correction goes in the log below and
        stays there. Shipped code that breaks is mine. "The model wrote that part" isn't a defense
        I get to use, and I wouldn't want it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># enforcement</h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
        Nothing enforces any of this except me and the public record of whether I keep it. That's
        thin, and it's what every personal policy amounts to. The record is the point.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="py-5 border-t border-[var(--color-line)]">
      <h2 class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]"># log</h2>
      {
        log.map((e) => (
          <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-dim)] mt-2 leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-mono text-sm text-[var(--color-ink)]">{e.version} · {e.date}</span> · {e.body}
          </p>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
